From 15521873b596ecc130eef41d774326ff36f47a46 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dom Cobley <popcornmix@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 13:43:59 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 2026/2064] VideoPlayer: hold a base view frame back until its
 partner arrives

hstack pairs its two inputs frame for frame, and each view of a multiview access
unit comes out of the decoder as a frame of its own, so the graph is fed one side
at a time and emits nothing until both have arrived. Which is fine while both do.
When the second eye stops arriving it is not: the decoder goes on tagging base
view frames with view 0, they queue behind a filter waiting for view 1, and on
the DRMPRIME path each one holds a DMA buffer, so the heap empties within a
couple of seconds, get_buffer2() fails for every frame after that, and the
picture goes black while the audio plays on. A log from a title that lost its
second eye mid-play shows ffmpeg's own warning at a hundred frames queued, then
seventeen hundred failed heap allocations to the end of playback.

A frame with no view id at all was already fed to both inputs, but that decides
at the wrong moment: whether a frame has a partner is not knowable until the next
one turns up. So hold each base view frame and let the frame after it say what to
do - a dependent view frame completes the pair, anything else means the held frame
is not getting one and it goes to both inputs as its own partner. The graph is
then never left half fed whatever the stream does, the title falls back to
playing flat rather than stopping, and one held frame always yields exactly one
pair, so the eyes stay paired across the transition either way.

The routing is the same for both decoders, so it lives in CMultiviewFramePairer
and both use it: DVDVideoCodecDRMPRIME feeds it from ProcessFilterIn(), and
DVDVideoCodecFFmpeg from FilterProcess(), which drains through it as well so that
both inputs see the end of the stream. A held frame is flushed at the end of the
stream so the last picture is not lost, and dropped with the graph.

Costs a frame of latency in the filter stage. GetPicture() already returns
without a picture for every base view frame of a working multiview stream, so
there is no new state for the caller to handle.

(cherry picked from commit 5bbe16b0299b3b95e09af395ef602499064b00ed)
